 saying  something is the case in denial it's  opposite saying that something is not  the case  and Aristotle clarifies that what we  mean when we talk about Cotta fascist is  a logos a statement a verbal or we could  say mental entity logos cotta thought  seacoast so a statement that is  affirming an affirmative statement  affirmation is affirmative statement  denial is  negation Allah or negating statement we  can say denying statement so this is a  very important point to keep in mind and  again keep it keep in mind as well that  Aristotle doesn't think this is just in  language but also can be in thought  which work which works like language  itself we can engage in kata faxes or  apophysis as activity now he also points  out that what is affirmed or denied what  the language is about right what is  affirmed or denied is not itself  affirmation or denial it's not as he  says a logos there could of course be  counter examples to this that he doesn't  consider for example when we're using  language meta linguistically and we are  talking about the language itself but  even then there's a there's a difference  going on it's true that it is they logos  but it's not being treated as a logos in  that case it is being treated perhaps as  a subject of which things are being  predicated or you know in this case  affirmed or denied or it's being treated  as we might say an object something that  language can be referring to this is an  interesting facet of language we can set  that aside and we can think instead  about the sort of examples that that  Aristotle uses the person sits the  person does not sit right the person  actually sitting is a thing that's  sitting down I'm just minding sitting  down because I don't have a chair right  not sitting of course could be a whole  bunch of things could be standing on  your head could be running around in  circles could be laying down on the  floor could be standing on one knee  could be getting yourself almost ready  to sit but the not sitting so there's  the denial sometimes isn't exactly clear  about about what is the case but it's  clear about what is not the case and so  Aristotle says that the person sitting  or  our the person not sitting it's not the  same thing as the statement about that  and why does he make this point because  there are cases where people lose sight  of this and they confuse the thing  itself that's being referred to or the  sets of things arranged in certain ways  with what is being said about them the  opposition that's happening here is  primarily between logos counterfeit  echoes you know speaking that is  asserting something and logosophical  speaking that is denying something those  are the things that are actually being  opposed here now he says that what is  affirmed or denied are in fact opposed  or opposites in the same way as the  statements themselves because the  statements themselves are in a certain  way mirroring the realities that are  that are under discussion or perhaps  being imagined right because as we're  going to see Aristotle is going to talk  about how we also do this with non  existent things in just a moment but  it's very important to keep this in mind  things can be opposed to each other  the person sits the person does not sit  that's referring to you know people in  the world or that you know the chalk is  white the chalk is not white right the  chalk is white is a cat a fascist the  chalk is not white is an oppo fascist  the chalk being white is not itself a  kind of fascist it is not an affirmation  it's just the quality in the chalk that  we're referring to in language we are  affirming whiteness of the truck and we  could deny whiteness of the truck as  well so those things are opposites in  the same sense you know if you somehow  the truck becomes different colored  maybe becomes transparent it's no longer  white right that's an opposite but  that's an opposite in a in the thing and  not in the affirmation and denial  Aristotle seems to say  that the verbal things can follow on the  reality of the the things and their  possibilities so he goes on and he says  something else that's really quite  interesting one of the two opposite  statements must be true and the other  false so if you think about it he is  sitting he is not sitting right if  somebody exists either they're sitting  at any given point in time or they're  not sitting and you can say well wait a  second what about people who you know  sit and then they get up okay now you're  bringing time into it and we can just  say at any given point in time look  either you're sitting or you're not  sitting either either you're living or  you're dead you know either the chalk is  white or it's not white notice what he  doesn't say either the chalk is white or  it's black no because there's other  possibilities for color right now  what about non-existent objects this is  where it gets really interesting  he says he's considering all these  different ways in which we can talk  about things either being true or not  true to return to affirmation and  negation of these we may say in all  cases that one must be false and the  other true whether the subject exists or  not how is that the case as you say says  well think about existing first if  Socrates really exists he is ill or not  ill must be true he is ill or not ill  must be false one of the others is true  the same if he does not exist why it  would seem that if he doesn't exist all  bets are off right we can imagine  anything we like about non-existent  things  Aristotle treats this in a kind of  hypothetical way he says provided he  does not exist and it's false to  pronounce he is ill he is he is not ill  however becomes true even if he doesn't  exist if we can say that one of them is  false by assumption then the other one  becomes true so he says thus that one  - must be true and the other be false in  all cases will hold these opposites only  which are in the same sense opposed as  affirmative and negative statements so  there's something really unique to this  type of opposition that imposes even on  non existent imaginary things that one  statement must be true and the other  statement must be false now it doesn't  mean that we automatically know which  one of them is true and which one of  them is false we might you know there  might be no way to decide that or you  might just decide it by Fiat this is  what often what happens when people tell  stories isn't it you know think about an  imaginary universe you know those of  science-fiction for example where people  play fast and loose with faster than  light travel you know if you think  faster than light travel is possible  because these aliens have it and they  share it with human beings then that  becomes true within that that set of  assumptions that fictional world and  faster than light travel is not possible  becomes a false statement and notice  that you can have people in those  fictional universes arguing about  whether it's true or false you know and  they may find it by experimentation but  only if it met that fictional universe  not out in the world that that were  inhabiting and presumably we share in  some sort of long continuity with  Aristotle so another very important type  of opposition cata fascist and appa  fascist affirmation and denial has to do  primarily with language or our thought  about things rather than the things  themselves although it refers to those  things
 

